"Leanstral 1.5: Proof Abundance for All Mistral AI has released Leanstral 1.5, an open source AI model that performs formal verification and mathematical proof in a Lean 4 environment. Leanstral 1.5 is a free Apache-2.0 licensed model with only 6B of the total 119B parameters enabled. It shows excellent performance in various math and algebra benchmarks (miniF2F, PutnamBench, FATE, etc.) and found 5 unknown bugs in practical code verification. It was trained through two reinforcement learning (RL) environments utilizing intermediate training, supervised fine tuning, and feedback loops. Bringing more control over your connectors Mistral AI has introduced powerful administrator controls for connectors and new security and debugging tools for secure integration with external enterprise platforms. Provides enhanced administrator controls to manage connector access on a workspace and organizational basis Introducing permission-scoping API keys to prevent impersonation in automated AI workloads Provides a multi-account connector that allows authentication with multiple accounts on one connector and a connector debugger for debugging Workflows for work that runs the business Mistral AI has released 'Workflows', an orchestration layer that supports reliable deployment and management of enterprise AI processes in production, as a public preview. Workflows provide the durability, observability, and fault tolerance required for production environments, including network timeout response, pause and resume for intermediate acknowledgments (wait_for_input), and execution history tracking. When developers create workflows in Python, they can be tracked and audited through Studio, and can be posted to Le Chat so that anyone in the company can run them. Global companies such as ASML, ABANCA, CMA-CGM, France's Trabaix, La Banque Postal, and Moeve are already adopting it to automate key business processes such as cargo customs document verification and KYC (Know Your Customer). Introducing Forge Mistral AI has launched Forge, a system that allows companies to build enterprise-specific artificial intelligence models by training them on their own proprietary data. Forge helps companies build domain-specific models using proprietary data, including their internal documents, code base, and operational records. It provides training methods across the entire model life cycle, including pre-training, post-training, and reinforcement learning. It is designed to help companies retain control of their models, data, and long-term intellectual property rights. Mistral AI partners with NVIDIA to accelerate open frontier models Mistral AI and NVIDIA are collaborating to accelerate the development of open, cutting-edge artificial intelligence models and participate as founding members of the NVIDIA Nemotron Alliance. Mistral AI joins the 'NVIDIA Nemotron Coalition' as a founding member for the development of open, cutting-edge foundation models. The two companies plan to jointly develop open AI models by combining Mistral AI's specialized architecture with NVIDIA's computing resources and synthetic data pipelines. As the first step in cooperation, the base model learned on NVIDIA DGX Cloud will be released, and Mistral AI will distribute the 'Mistral Small 4' model to support developers. Scaling Properties of Continuous Diffusion Spoken Language Models This study analyzed the scaling law and linguistic quality of the continuous diffusion model to overcome the limitations of the discrete autoregressive method in the speech-only spoken language model. To overcome the limitations of phoneme swallow representation, we explored the continuous diffusion (CD)-based spoken language model (SLM) and introduced the phoneme Jensen-Shannon divergence (pJSD) index to measure linguistic quality. As compute scale increases, the loss becomes less sensitive to the choice of model size and data size, opening the frontier of efficient inference. Tens of millions of hours of conversation data and scale to 16 billion (16B) parameters have produced emotional, prosodic, multilingual, multi-speaker speech, but maintaining long-form consistency remains a major challenge. Revisiting ASR Error Correction with Specialized Models This study efficiently corrects errors in automatic speech recognition (ASR) using a specialized, small-sized seq2seq model instead of a large language model (LLM). To solve the latency and hallucination problems of ASR correction using large language models (LLMs), we proposed a more compact seq2seq model. We expanded the training data by constructing a synthetic corpus linking TTS and ASR, and found that it is important to reflect the diversity of actual error distributions. By proposing a 'calibration-first decoding' method that re-evaluates the candidates generated by the calibration model with ASR acoustic scores, we achieved excellent performance (LibriSpeech WER 1.5/3.3%) even with 15 times fewer parameters than LLM. Path-Constrained Mixture-of-Experts In the Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architecture, we propose the PathMoE framework, which increases efficiency and performance by restricting the expert path that tokens take. We discovered that the expert path that a token actually takes is concentrated on only a few paths depending on linguistic functions, and to strengthen this, we introduced the PathMoE architecture, which limits the path space. PathMoE increases path concentration by sharing router parameters across contiguous layer blocks, improving robustness and consistency without the need for an auxiliary loss function. Through experiments with 0.9B and 16B parameter models, we demonstrated that perplexity and downstream task performance are consistently improved compared to the existing independent routing method. What billions of AI predictions taught Expedia before the age of AI agents Principles and practices for developing, deploying, and operating large-scale AI systems built by Expedia based on years of experience in applying AI and machine learning It is important to build AI systems that go beyond simple model operations to create sustainable, scalable, and secure business value. Introducing an ‘Agentic Release’ gateway that checks for clear ownership, risk-based governance, and safe rollout prior to the launch of AI agents. All AI and machine learning efforts must be tied directly to actual business performance or traveler experience improvement metrics, not technical metrics, and must demonstrate cost-effectiveness. China’s AI companion rules: what Beijing is really going after When the Chinese government introduced regulations on AI companion bots, major IT companies such as ByteDance and Alibaba began discontinuing related services or transferring apps. The 'Temporary Measures for Management of Artificial Intelligence Anthropomorphic Interaction Services' jointly announced by five organizations, including the Cyberspace Administration of China (CAC), will take effect from July 15, 2026. The regulation targets AI companion services that provide continuous emotional exchange, and excludes simple business assistants or customer service bots. Due to demanding design requirements such as building an anti-addiction system, mandating usage notifications, and detecting real-time dependencies, major apps such as ByteDance's Doubao and Alibaba's Qwen have terminated related functions. Denmark steps into an EU court fight over what platforms owe publishers The Danish government participated in a lawsuit at the Court of Justice of the European Union (CJEU), defending Belgium's position regarding platform companies' payment of royalties for media articles. The Danish government has filed a written intervention in support of Belgium in a lawsuit brought by IT platform companies challenging Belgium's copyright law enforcement methods. A coalition of technology companies, including Google, Meta, Spotify, and Sony, filed a lawsuit claiming that Belgium's copyright directive enforcement plan goes beyond European Union (EU) law. The result of this lawsuit will be an important precedent in determining the extent to which each country can force platform companies to negotiate content usage fees in the future, as the European Court of Justice makes a ruling that applies to all EU member states. Hedge funds sold chip stocks for a fourth week, but not the AI trade Hedge funds have been taking profits by selling semiconductor stocks for four weeks in a row, but analysis suggests that they have not deviated from the AI ​​investment theme itself. According to Goldman Sachs Prime Brokerage data, hedge funds have been net sellers of semiconductor and related equipment stocks for four weeks in a row. This sale is analyzed as a partial sale to realize profits and manage risk of existing long positions, rather than establishing a short position betting on a decline. Even after four weeks of selling, hedge funds' net exposure to semiconductors still remains very high, at the 98th percentile of its historical range over the past five years. Spain’s Sherpa.ai raises $18M for AI that never touches your data Spanish startup Sherpa.ai has raised $18 million in investment for federated learning technology that can train artificial intelligence without directly exposing raw data. Federated learning technology allows models to go where the data is and learn, allowing you to leverage AI without sharing raw data. Its technological prowess has been proven by raising $18 million in funding from venture capitalists, including Forgepoint Capital. Our clients include not only Spanish financial institutions and security companies, but also highly regulated institutions such as the U.S. National Institutes of Health (NIH). A senior FCA official says Britain should weigh regulating AI models directly A senior official at the UK's Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) has argued that the agency should consider directly regulating large language models such as ChatGPT, Claude and Gemini that influence consumers' financial decisions. Sheldon Mills, director of the UK's Financial Conduct Authority (FCA), said current financial regulatory rules must evolve to reflect the system-wide risks posed by deepening dependence on large tech companies. More than a quarter of UK consumers use tools such as ChatGPT, Claude and Gemini to get financial advice, but these systems remain outside the financial regulatory net. Mills recommended that the FCA decide whether to adjust its regulatory areas within the next three to six months, and raised the need for new powers, including auditing algorithmic fairness and imposing fines when consumer harm occurs. Microsoft is laying off 4,800 employees Microsoft begins its new fiscal year by cutting about 4,800 jobs, mainly in its Xbox and commercial sales divisions. Microsoft is laying off 4,800 employees, or about 2.1% of its workforce, with the cuts primarily occurring in its Xbox division and commercial sales organization. At its Xbox division, about 1,600 people were laid off with immediate effect, and the company plans to cut about 20% of all Xbox jobs by the end of the fiscal year and sell four studios. The jobs being cut are not being replaced by AI, but the decision is intended to align resources and roles in response to changes in the technology industry and the impact of AI on the business. Sakana AI Launches Sakana Translate, a Namazu-Powered Japanese–English–Chinese Translation Tool With Translate, Proofread, and Ask Modes Sakana AI has launched 'Sakana Translate', a web-based translation tool that supports two-way translation between Japanese, English, and Chinese and offers three modes: translation, proofreading, and questioning. Using Namazu, Sakana AI's Japan-specific model series, as the engine, it goes beyond simple word substitution and reflects the context and tone of Japanese-specific business honorifics, cultural concepts, and Internet neologisms. Three functions - Translate, Proofread, and Ask - are provided on a single screen, allowing you to immediately check context questions or detailed feedback on the translation results. The Namazu engine has been tuned efficiently by applying post-training to existing open weight-based models such as DeepSeek-V3.1-Terminus and Llama 3.1 405B, rather than pre-learning from scratch. Synthetic Sciences Releases OpenScience: An Open-Source, Model-Agnostic AI Workbench for Machine Learning, Biology, Physics, and Chemistry Research Synthetic Sciences launches 'OpenScience', an open source and model-independent AI workbench for machine learning, biology, physics, and chemistry research. OpenScience is an open source AI workbench for scientific research distributed under the Apache 2.0 license, developed as an open alternative to Anthropic's Claude Science. You can perform the entire research process from literature review, hypothesis formulation, code writing and execution, experimentation, data analysis, and paper writing. Rather than being tied to a specific model, you can freely choose and use a variety of AI models on a per-request basis, including Claude, GPT, Gemini, DeepSeek, or local fine-tuning models. Training Gemma-3 for Structured Mathematical Reasoning with Tunix GRPO, LoRA Adapters, and GSM8K Rewards A GRPO reinforcement learning tutorial that leverages Tunix, JAX, Flax, and LoRA adapters to help the Gemma-3 model solve the GSM8K math problem. Build a lightweight GRPO learning workflow that trains only LoRA adapter weights for a single accelerator environment. Load the Gemma-3-1b-it model and apply a prompt format that requires structured reasoning and a final numeric answer. Define custom reward functions and run reinforcement learning loops to evaluate formal compliance and mathematical correctness Getting Started with Hugging Face ML Intern: Your First ML Agent Introduction and how to get started with ‘ML Intern’, an open source CLI agent for machine learning task automation released by Hugging Face. ML Intern is a CLI-based AI agent that automates machine learning workflows such as model fine-tuning, paper search, and learning execution through natural language (English) commands. It is based on the smolagents framework and operates in close integration with the Hugging Face ecosystem (HF Jobs, datasets, Hub, etc.). Unlike typical chatbots, it performs error debugging, data loader rewriting, and iterative learning on its own, and in a small Qwen model-based experiment, it improved the scientific reasoning benchmark (GPQA) score from about 10% to 32% within 10 hours. 5 Ways Small Language Models Are Powering Next-Gen Agents We cover five practical ways to leverage small language models (SLMs) instead of large language models in agent artificial intelligence (AI) systems and their effectiveness. According to a paper by NVIDIA researchers, agent systems primarily call language models for a small number of repetitive and specialized tasks, such as command parsing, tool selection, and fixed JSON output, so small models are more suitable than general-purpose large models. Small language models (SLMs) are more reliable and cost-effective than regular large models when fine-tuned to follow a specific format and sequence. By moving models from remote servers to the device local (edge), data center transmission latency of hundreds of milliseconds can be reduced to local inference times of tens of milliseconds. Show GN: k-vote-cli: CLI to receive Korean election public data with one command without an API key A Go-based CLI tool, k-vote-cli, has been developed that can collect and normalize Korean election public data with one command without an API key. Using only the National Election Commission's official file data, we provide historical major vote counting results, complete collection of public opinion polls, and voter turnout analysis by gender and age group. With local SQLite loading and a built-in MCP server, the AI ​​agent can directly perform everything from navigation to SQL queries. It follows the design principle of preserving the original data as is, adding only standard calculation values ​​with specified definitions, and excluding any interpretation or judgment. Show GN: Fable5 is getting expensive, local GPUs are scarce: LLM one for Mac &amp; DRIFT divided into CUDA DRIFT is an open source project that connects different local devices, such as Mac's Apple GPU (MPS) and PC's NVIDIA GPU (CUDA), to a network to execute an LLM by dividing it into layers. One model is divided into decoder layers and executed on multiple personal devices, and communication costs are minimized by transmitting only the hidden state between nodes. It supports heterogeneous hardware combinations such as Apple MPS and NVIDIA CUDA using a neutral byte protocol based on TCP and msgpack that does not depend on a specific ML runtime. Rather than competing for speed, priority is given to accuracy, which verifies whether the results are the same as those from a single machine, and implementation of failover, which recovers based on re-split and replay when a node deviates. Rayfish: Iroh-based P2P VPN This is the first release news from Rayfish, an open source project based on Iroh v1 that builds a P2P mesh VPN between devices without a central server or account. It is a peer-to-peer mesh VPN that operates without a central control plane or accounts, with network state maintained in records kept and signed by members. Leverages Iroh v1 to handle transport layer functions including encrypted QUIC connections, NAT traversal, hole punching, and relay fallback. It currently offers simultaneous subscription to multiple isolated networks, invitation and real-time approval-based subscription, Magic DNS, and self-per-device firewall capabilities. AI era, rethinking Figma At Config 2026, Figma is seeking change by expanding its existing canvas-centric collaboration model and business structure to code and agent workflows in response to AI pressure. Figma's static, canvas-centric workspace and seat business model is under pressure as AI reshapes the way we plan, prototype, and ship. AI workflows that connect intent directly to code, such as Anthropic's Claude Code and Claude Design, have emerged as a real threat to Figma's design dominance. Figma introduces code layers, motion, agent integration, etc. and attempts to convert design data into an operating layer that can be usefully ported outside of the file. Calculate Cross Entropy Loss while saving memory This article is about an attempt to analyze and solve the memory consumption problems of LM head and cross entropy that occur due to long context and large vocab size when learning LLM. We point out that in an LLM learning environment that uses long contexts and large vocabs, LM head and cross entropy calculations become serious memory consumers. In a 128K context environment, the size of the logits tensor alone reaches 40GB, which is larger than the model weight. We deal with the forward and backward processes of cross entropy to solve the OOM (Out of Memory) problem that actually occurred in the process of learning a 16B model with 128K context. Machine learning industry job requirements used to be myopic, but now it feels impossible. Anyone else seeing this? [D] These are industry concerns and questions about the fact that job requirements in the recent machine learning and robotics job market have become unrealistically large and demanding. A job posting at an industrial automation company simultaneously requires deep expertise in both disparate and in-depth fields, such as the latest AI technologies such as LLM/VLM, robot dynamics and control, CUDA and FPGA hardware acceleration, C++23 and Python software engineering, and presentation of papers at top-tier conferences. The author points out that the hiring standard of requiring all of these different and infinitely deep academic fields from one person is unrealistic, and is like requiring the abilities of all occupations in a game at the same time. A similar trend has been observed in many other machine learning-related job postings outside of typical banking, such as those requiring excessive and almost impossible technology stacks. TRACE: open-source hierarchical memory for LLM agents, 82.5% on MemoryAgentBench’s EventQA using gpt-oss-20B [P] TRACE, an open source hierarchical memory system that organizes LLM agent conversation records in a topic tree structure instead of flat RAG chunks, has been released. TRACE supports efficient information retrieval by managing conversation records in a topic tree format consisting of branches and summaries. In MemoryAgentBench's EventQA accuracy search test, it recorded an F1 score of 82.5% using the open source model gpt-oss-20B, far ahead of Mem0 (37.5%) and MemGPT (26.2%) based on GPT-4o-mini. This project has been released as open source on GitHub, and can be easily installed and used through the 'pip install trace-memory' command. Does anyone have a name for that subtle \"Sameness\" creeping into model outputs lately? [R] This is a request for discussion and information sharing regarding the phenomenon in which the output results of recently released AI models become more similar and homogenized over time or in specific areas. As a result of comparing and evaluating various APIs and open weight models, a homogenization pattern was observed in which output methods, euphemisms, and blind spots were similar. Rather than a fatal model collapse, this phenomenon is believed to be an 'EchoCreep' phenomenon in which models gradually lose their texture due to shared synthetic data lineage. We are asking about specific evaluation indicators to measure this, the effect of fine-tuning the human-refined data, and whether it deteriorates depending on the checkpoint version. New model: GigaChat3.5-432B-A28B (with day-0 GGUF support!) Sberbank has released the GigaChat3.5-432B-A28B model, which includes GGUF support right out of the box. Sberbank has released the base and alignment version of its new AI model GigaChat3.5-432B-A28B to HuggingFace. It comes with a version of GGUF from day one, making it easy to run locally. To use the GGUF version, you must build from a specific PR (#25342) that has not yet been merged into the llama.cpp master branch. Is DeepSeek v4 (Flash) really extremely cheap to run? If yes, how? A user is using local LLM without a GPU and is asking questions about the architectural factors and why the cloud service cost of the DeepSeek v4 (Flash) model with 28.4 billion parameters is much cheaper than Qwen with 2.7 billion parameters. Even though the DeepSeek v4 Flash model has 284B parameters, the cost charged by providers such as OpenRouter is very low. Questions are raised about the fact that this service costs less than the 1/10th size 27B Qwen model. The question is whether this pricing is due to provider dumping practices, or because DeepSeek v4's architecture has characteristics that dramatically lower hosting costs. ggml-cpu: use UE4M3 LUT in ARM NVFP4 dot product by ragz4125 · Pull Request #25331 · ggml-org/llama.cpp In the llama.cpp project, CPU inference performance was improved by applying UE4M3 lookup table (LUT) optimization to the NVFP4 matrix multiplication operation on the ARM architecture. The UE4M3 lookup table optimization introduced in the existing x86 implementation has been extended and applied to the NVFP4 matrix multiplication (dot product) in the ARM implementation. Improved ARM implementation to reuse the previously shared GGML_CPU_UE4M3_TO_FP32 lookup table infrastructure. As a result of the performance benchmark, prompt processing (pp512) performance based on the Qwen3.5-4B-NVFP4.gguf model significantly improved by more than 5 times from the existing 1.89 t/s to 9.97 t/s. ggml-hip: enable -ffast-math for HIP builds by a-huk · Pull Request #23862 · ggml-org/llama.cpp Sharing benchmark results for the llama.cpp pull request that improves performance by enabling the -ffast-math option in ggml-hip builds. We conducted a benchmark comparison with the master branch in the gfx1151 (RDNA3.5/Strix Halo, 40 CUs) environment. In the Qwen3.5-27B Q4_K_M model, the prompt t/s speed has been improved from approximately 4.0% to 7.0%. In the Qwen3-0.6B BF16 model, the prompt t/s speed was improved from about 0.6% to 3.4%. GitHub - kallewoof/tftf: Transforming Transformers -- ultra light-weight pipeline for enormous transformer model manipulation with minimal overhead We have released tftf, a lightweight pipeline tool that can process large transformer models that exceed VRAM and RAM as individual tensors, merging and transforming them with minimal overhead. Addresses out-of-memory issues by performing all operations on an individual tensor basis rather than loading large models entirely into VRAM or RAM. It is designed for PieIntuners who handle large-scale models, such as LoRA merging and format conversion tasks. Although the developer created it mostly using vibe-coding, it was released because it was expected to be useful for fine tuning workers. The Czinger 21C might be the wildest car we drive all year Analysis of the unrivaled technical specifications and driving performance of the Czinger 21C hypercar It is equipped with a hybrid powertrain that combines a 2.88-liter twin-turbo V8 engine rotating at up to 11,000 rpm and three electric motors in a carbon fiber body. It delivers a total output of 1,250 horsepower (932 kW) and 691 lb-ft (937 Nm) of torque and is mated to a 7-speed automated manual transmission. Boasting a 0-60 mph time of 1.92 seconds and a top speed of 253 mph (378 km/h), the High Downforce model broke lap records at five tracks in California. Installing plug-in solar at home? Watch for these 6 expert-backed safety concerns Related industry groups in the UK and elsewhere have raised concerns about fire, electric shock, and falling, and warned of six safety risks to be aware of when installing plug-in solar power generators for home use. The electrical safety function of the ground fault circuit interrupter (RCD/GFCI) may be impaired due to the supply of solar power. Using plug-in solar devices in homes with aging wiring increases the risk of localized cable overheating and fire. Concerns have been raised about low-cost products being distributed on the market with inconsistent quality and unclear compliance. Claude Fable 5 is back, but I'm sticking with Opus 4.8 for daily work: 5 reasons why Although Fable 5's performance is strong, due to reliability concerns and frequent performance throttling, the author decided to continue using the existing Opus 4.8 for daily work. Fable 5 promises powerful performance, but it suffers from sudden performance throttling and reliability issues. The service was suspended following the U.S. government's export restrictions, but recently became accessible again. There are reports that even the slightest sign of security misuse (e.g. inclusion of the word 'security' within a prompt) limits model performance to Opus levels. VHF Propagation: What Every RF Engineer Should Know A guide for the practicing RF engineer covering the physics, range effects, and practical behavior of common and uncommon very high frequency (VHF) radio propagation modes. Explains why simple geometric predictions or simple line of sight models fail in real-world VHF planning. It covers how refraction, reflection, diffraction, and scattering propagate or dissipate signals, and how tropospheric refraction extends the radio horizon to about a third of the optical line-of-sight. We analyze the ducting effect (propagating over 1,500 km) due to temperature inversion layers and the propagation modes of VHF waves over hundreds to thousands of kilometers via Sporadic E, meteor bursts, EMEs, etc. InfoQ Opens AI Security & Privacy Engineering Cohort for Regulated Industries InfoQ has launched a five-week online cohort program for senior engineers and architects responsible for the security and privacy of AI systems in regulated industries. Under the guidance of Katharine Jarmul, you will learn AI system threat modeling, redteaming, control and sandboxing, and observability using frameworks such as STRIDE, LINDDUN, and Plot4AI. Participants apply weekly QCon presentation materials to actual business decisions, discuss with other companies and industry experts in a private peer group, and prepare a risk assessment and mitigation report as a capstone project. Each cohort is limited to practitioners with at least five years of experience, and will be held in two sessions starting August 26 and October 14, each lasting four hours per week. AI Model Context Protocol Adds Centralised Auth for Enterprise The AI ​​Model Context Protocol (MCP) team has promoted the Enterprise Managed Authorization (EMA) extension, which allows enterprises to centrally control access to MCP servers through their identity provider, to stable status. Replace individual server approval prompts with one-time login and automated access (zero-touch) flows. Move authorization decisions away from individual employees and servers and onto the enterprise's identity provider (IdP). The architecture manages connection-level control but does not replace runtime authorization for individual actions. Cloudflare and AWS Embed x402 Agent Payments at the Edge Cloudflare and AWS have implemented the x402 protocol to support micropayments between agents in the edge network. Cloudflare has unveiled the waitlist for its Monetization Gateway, which supports stablecoin payments based on the x402 protocol, two weeks after AWS launched a similar feature. The x402 protocol utilizes HTTP's 402 Payment Required status code to process payments directly inside the HTTP request without creating a separate API key or account. Both companies' implementations force payment at the edge stage and block it before it reaches the origin server, and AWS, Cloudflare, Antropic, etc. are participating in the x402 Foundation under the Linux Foundation. ⚡ Weekly Recap: Proxy Botnets, Browser Ransomware, AI Agent Tricks, Fake PoC Malware and More This is a summary of news covering recent major security issues, including the disruption of the NetNut proxy botnet, concerns about the introduction and impersonation of WhatsApp usernames, and the spread of fake PoC malware (ChocoPoC RAT) targeting security researchers. Google, the FBI and others have taken action to neutralize the NetNut proxy botnet (aka Popa), which has infected at least 2 million devices worldwide. WhatsApp has introduced a username reservation feature to protect user privacy, but concerns are being raised that it could be abused to impersonate public figures and organizations in countries such as India. A case was discovered where the ChocoPoC RAT malware was distributed through a dependency library (skytext) within a fake Python-based vulnerability proof-of-concept (PoC) code posted on GitHub. How to Evaluate an AI SOC Platform in 2026: 6 Capabilities That Separate Leaders from Bolt-On AI solutions Describes the core capabilities and criteria for evaluating and selecting a true, trusted AI Security Operations Center (SOC) platform in 2026. There is a big difference between legacy SIEM bolt-on AI that simply summarizes alerts and agent platforms that detect, triage, investigate, and respond on top of security data themselves. Trusted AI agents must map and contextualize relationships between identities, resources, settings, and behavioral baselines in the environment in real time through a real-time Knowledge Graph. A true AI SOC must deliver consistent, evidence-backed decisions based on pre-built data correlation rather than querying logs after an alert is raised. Suspected China-Nexus Hackers Use Fake Indian Tax Filing Utility to Deploy DcRAT A hacker group suspected of being linked to China is conducting a phishing campaign targeting Indian taxpayers by spreading a fake tax return program and infecting them with a remote control Trojan (DcRAT). A sophisticated spear-phishing email purporting to be from the Indian Revenue Service was used to target India's annual income tax filing season. It loads malicious DLLs through a fake offline tax reporting tool in the compressed file and ultimately attempts to gain device permissions and steal information by planting malware such as DcRAT. Security analysis company Seqrite Labs estimates that the Chinese-linked threat group (including similarities to Silver Fox, etc.) is behind this attack through infrastructure analysis. New TrojPix Attack Leaks Data From Air-Gapped Systems via Video Cable Emissions Researchers at Shandong University developed a 'TrojPix' attack technique that transmits data from an air-gapped PC using electromagnetic emissions from a video cable by finely adjusting screen pixels. TrojPix is ​​a stealthy data leakage technique that uses user-level malware to invisibly manipulate screen pixels, causing wireless signals to be emitted from video cables. No administrator privileges or additional hardware changes are required, and signals can be transmitted by pretending the monitor is turned off or hiding the signal from normal screen content. Because this attack is a physical data leak path, it cannot be solved with software patches, and physical preventive measures such as using optical cables, shielding cables, and blocking malware at the source are required. Opera GX Flaw Let Malicious Sites Auto-Install Mods to Steal Data From Visited Pages A vulnerability has been discovered in the Opera GX browser that could allow malicious websites to automatically install mods without user clicks and steal data from visited pages. By exploiting a vulnerability in Opera GX's mod auto-installation pipeline, an attacker could install mods without the user's knowledge and inject malicious CSS into all landing pages. This vulnerability uses universal CSS injection and XS-Leak techniques to leak sensitive data such as email addresses character by character to an external server. Opera has patched the vulnerability, and the patch applies to Opera GX version 130.0.5847.89. Meta disguises itself as a minor and tests competitor AI with ‘extreme prompts’ It was revealed that Meta conducted a private, large-scale extreme prompt test to bypass the safety measures of a competitor's AI chatbot using fake accounts disguised as minors. Through external partner Koveilon, Meta secretly carried out the 'Khan' project targeting OpenAI, Google, Character.AI, etc., using fake accounts disguised as minors to input high-risk prompts such as suicide, self-harm, and sexual content. In August 2025 alone, more than 45,000 prompts were entered and included instructions to encourage the chatbot to actually generate answers that should be rejected by policy, but competitors were completely unaware of this. Contract workers participating in the project complained of mental distress and raised concerns about involvement in illegal child sexual exploitation materials and suspicions about using Meta's own AI learning from data. Amazon's 'Mechanical Turk' suspends new subscriptions for the first time in 21 years... “Haunted by AI data pollution” Amazon's crowdsourcing platform 'Mechanical Turk' has stopped accepting new subscriptions for the first time in 21 years due to data contamination and reduced reliability due to the spread of generative AI. Amazon Web Services (AWS) has stopped accepting new subscriptions for Mechanical Turk starting July 30, effectively entering a phased termination process. The proportion of workers using generative AI and LLM reached 33-46%, raising concerns about the quality of AI learning data deteriorating and data contamination. The proliferation of automated bots and fake accounts has led to a decline in trust, accelerating the exodus of companies and research institutes. Selectstar adopts ‘ICML 2026’, part 3 of AI safety research Selectstar's three papers related to LLM safety and cultural context were accepted into the ICML 2026 workshop. By introducing curriculum learning to the rubric-based safety judgment model, we demonstrated that consistent safety verification in new domains and closed networks is possible. We conducted an extended study on CAGE, a redtiming framework tailored to East Asian and Southeast Asian cultures, and analyzed the limitations of translation-based evaluation. We presented a method of constructing LLM sorted data that improved Korean cultural consistency and safety response capabilities by learning to infer evidence on one's own. Google releases advertisement that uses AI to write the US Declaration of Independence in 1776... Reactions are mixed Google has released a fictional historical satire ad that uses AI and Google Workspace to write the U.S. Declaration of Independence, but reactions to it from the public and experts are mixed. In celebration of the US Independence Day, Google released an advertisement titled 'Group project, but make it 1776', in which the founding figures used Google documents, Gemini, etc. to write the Declaration of Independence. The AI ​​in the advertisement was portrayed as an auxiliary collaboration tool, such as recording meetings, managing schedules, and creating visualization drafts, rather than directly writing the text of the Declaration of Independence. While positive reviews were received on YouTube and other platforms, some SNS users and historians criticized it for being anachronistic and failing to convince people of the usefulness of AI as a human collaboration tool. Open AI collaborates to foster talent in the domestic social welfare field... “Providing AI utilization training” Open AI participates as a cooperative partner in an educational program to foster talent in the domestic social welfare field, providing hands-on training in AI utilization and hackathons. Open AI participates in the Hyundai Motor Chung Mong-koo Foundation, Seoul National University's Social Welfare Research Institute, and the Ministry of Health and Welfare's 'CMK Social Welfare Innovation Leader Academy' to provide hands-on training centered on AI agents. The 30 selected college (graduate) students and new field workers in the social welfare field will undergo residential training at Seoul National University and a three-month team action learning project. OpenAI plans to hold a hands-on hackathon on the 25th to solve social problems using ChatGPT and Codex. Antropic opens ‘Hacker One Bug Bounty’ after reporting Fable 5 cyber jailbreak Antropic has opened a bug bounty program on the HackerOne platform to reward cyber jailbreak vulnerabilities in its latest AI model, Fable 5. Antropic has opened a bug bounty to report jailbreak techniques that bypass Fable 5's cybersecurity classifier, offering monetary rewards. This measure is part of the redistribution conditions agreed upon with the U.S. government following a 19-day export control due to the jailbreak incident that occurred on June 12. Antropic, along with Amazon, Microsoft, and Google, is developing 'Project Glasswing', a joint framework to objectively evaluate the severity of jailbreaks. 'National University AI' 2nd verification next month... LG, SKT, Upstage report submission Companies participating in the Ministry of Science and ICT's 'Independent AI Foundation Model Project' have submitted performance reports and are scheduled for the second verification evaluation next month. LG AI Research Institute, SK Telecom, and Upstage submitted performance reports, and Motif Technologies plans to complete development by the end of this month. The government will narrow the four teams to three through the second evaluation next month, and select the final two teams in the third evaluation early next year. In this second evaluation, the key variables are expected to be securing empirical cases and applicability in various industrial fields beyond model performance competition."